sections.body:
A `Set` is a collection that keeps every value **only once**. If you add the same value a second time, it ignores it:

```js
const visited = new Set();
visited.add("Mars");
visited.add("Venus");
visited.add("Mars");              // already there, not added
console.log(visited.size);        // 2
console.log(visited.has("Mars")); // true
visited.delete("Venus");
```

Its most common use: removing duplicates from an array. `new Set(array)` drops the duplicates and `[...set]` turns it back into an array:

```js
const logs = ["Mars", "Moon", "Mars", "Moon", "Venus"];
const unique = [...new Set(logs)]; // ['Mars', 'Moon', 'Venus']
```

A `Map` also holds key-value pairs like an object, but works with its own methods: `set`, `get`, `has`, `delete` and `size`.

```js
const cargo = new Map();
cargo.set("water", 40);
cargo.set("fuel", 25);
console.log(cargo.get("water")); // 40
console.log(cargo.size);         // 2
cargo.set("water", 35);          // same key: updates the value

for (const [item, amount] of cargo) {
  console.log(item, amount);
}
```

When to use which? For something with fixed fields (a player, a ship) use an **object**; for things that are constantly added, removed and counted (a word counter, inventory amounts) a **Map** is handy.

